azgor wrote:
> [2]: Nethack has sex and booze and demons and other things that would
> earn it a Mature rating if it was sold in stores. Why doesn't it also
> have tobacco? I would like a pipe and "pipe weed" (as per Middle Earth
> legend). Smoking should have some benefit for wizards like making it
> easier to read spellbooks or to regenerate PW. --rogza

We already have potions of hallucination with more special effects and
witty messages than most games could even dream of having.

> [I was imagining smoking as a special type of resting.  I didn't
> imagine one would smoke and melee monsters at the same time.  Perhaps,
> "Your pipe goes out," if you attack something or even if you move at
> all. -az]

Or it makes one confused or stunned while it lasts?

Wizards running out of power in the early game (and in the late game
with the amulet) is something that's annoying, but may be within the
design intended by the DevTeam.  If wizards were never in danger of
running out of power then they'd definitely be overpowered, as they'd
have all the benefits of magic without one of the most substantial
drawbacks.  So it really depends on what they think about it.

> [3]: there should be a #smoke extended command --AskHL

There's enough other special-purpose commands (like #sit, I can only
think of one use for that in the entire game) in Nethack that it
definitely has precedent.

> [4]: polymorph applied to pet should be permanent as it already is, but
> the pet should keep its XP lvl. This would 1.prevent people abusing
> polytraps to get pets way above what they could tame (by getting them
> to step on it over and over) and 2.safeguard hardwork levelling up a
> pet --Xarann

This is a weird thing, as that is indeed how it works in Dungeons &
Dragons.  But I still like Nethack's current system better, although to
explain why would take a good number of words from the ol' Word Box,
and I used up my current supply last night writing the column.

